WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.910
- Forget Wonder Woman's
golden lassos and tiaras.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.430
The Amazons of ancient myth
were much more gruesome

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.410
than any DC comic heroine.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.400
According to the legends,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.740
they were man-hating,
baby-killing war machines

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.060
who cut off their own breasts

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.220
just so they could shoot
their arrows better.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.530
Amazons were the
fearsome, worthy opponents

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.970
that male heroes had to defeat

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.830
to prove their battle prowess.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.380
- But were these warrior women even real?

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.810
Images of fiercely independent Amazons

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.090
could be found on countless
vases, sculptures,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.830
and buildings from antiquity.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.960
Yet over time, they faded into myth

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.420
along with the dryads and centaurs.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.910
Scholars have debated their
existence for decades,

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.950
but recent archeological discoveries

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.078
might have finally solved this mystery.

00:00:41.078 --> 00:00:43.745
(lilting music)

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.220
- [Moiya] TV shows like
"Xena: Warrior Princess"

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.630
and comics like "Wonder Woman"

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.450
paint a seemingly contradictory
picture of Amazons

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.640
as terrifying but attractive,

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.280
isolated from the world but
still compassionate enough

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.380
to aid those they deem worthy.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.930
Modern media also gives
the distinct impression

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.170
that Amazons were merely
figures of fantastical myth.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.330
These depictions surely
wouldn't have sat well

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.300
with ancient Greek writers
who saw Amazons as very real,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.310
but savagely brutish people.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.800
The oldest surviving written
mention of the Amazons

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.283
can be found in Homer's "The
Iliad," penned around 700 BCE,

00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:20.610
a time when the boundary
between myth and history

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.557
was so fuzzy it was almost non-existent.

00:01:23.557 --> 00:01:25.980
"The Iliad" tells the
story of the Trojan War,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.320
which supposedly happened five centuries

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.900
before Homer's time,
though modern archeologists

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.480
are skeptical that a
war of quite that scale

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.940
actually happened the way Homer described.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.130
Still, the Amazons' presence in the story

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.590
suggests that they were in
the Greek cultural zeitgeist

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.570
as far back as the Bronze Age.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.130
Recent findings from grave sites

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.920
in parts of Russia and
Ukraine have scholars thinking

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.900
that his descriptions of the warrior women

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.580
may have also been exaggerated.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.290
But first, let's take a closer look

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.410
at how Amazons have been
depicted in literature and art.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.570
- [Emily] The great Heracles
was rumored to have fought

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.580
the Amazons and emerged victorious

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.280
during the ninth of his 12 famous labors.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.730
The Amazon Queen Hippolyta
wore a leather girdle, or belt,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.340
that was supposedly gifted
to her by her father Ares

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.470
for being her people's fiercest fighter.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.650
King Eurystheus, who
assigned Heracles his labors,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.960
had a daughter who
coveted Hippolyta's belt,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.150
so Heracles was sent to retrieve it.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.850
He sailed with a small
armada across the Black Sea

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.692
to the Amazons' distant
and mysterious homeland.

00:02:23.692 --> 00:02:25.620
When he arrived, Hippolyta agreed

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.780
to give him the girdle without bloodshed,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.940
but the goddess Hera had other ideas.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.910
She convinced the Amazons that
their queen was in danger,

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.370
so they attacked Heracles' forces.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.770
Hippolyta was struck down
in the ensuing battle,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.900
and Heracles took her belt back to Tiryns

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.220
and its greedy king.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.560
Despite his many impressive opponents,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.480
the moment where Heracles
defeats Hippolyta

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.600
is one of his most oft-depicted
laborers in classical art.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.810
What a convenient way for
artists to uphold male heroism

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.270
while simultaneously reminding
women what can happen

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.640
if they stray from their designated roles.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.440
In other later versions of the story,

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.010
either Hippolyta or another
powerful Amazon named Antiope

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.490
was stolen away to Athens
to be King Theseus' bride.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.280
The Amazons besieged
Athens to bring her home,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.400
but were ultimately defeated
by this mighty city state.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.860
This battle known as the Amazonomachy

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.440
is featured on the west
side of the Parthenon.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.650
- In Homer's epic poem and other
accounts of the Trojan War,

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.860
the Amazons were led into battle
by their Queen Penthesilea

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.110
to fight for Troy after their hero Hector

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.760
was slain by Achilles.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.040
Penthesilea's motivation
for joining the war effort

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.470
is unclear, perhaps
she was repaying a debt

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.960
to her old foe Priam,
the last king of Troy,

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.540
or maybe she and her army
were paid mercenaries

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.760
hired by a desperate monarch.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.070
Some say she was seeking
retribution through glory

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.200
for murdering one of her fellow Amazons.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.610
Whatever her reasoning,

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.040
the story of Amazons in the Trojan War

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.140
ends much like every other Greek tale

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.970
featuring these fearsome warriors.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.800
They fought with enough bravery and skill

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.180
to diminish the Greek ranks,

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.670
but not enough to turn the tide of war.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:59.220
Queen Penthesilea herself
was eventually slain

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.720
by the Greek hero Achilles,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.780
who according to some writers
like Arctinus of Miletus,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.460
fell in love with her beauty

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.653
after her helmet was removed in death.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:09.806
You're a worthy opponent,

00:04:09.806 --> 00:04:11.790
but no match for the great Achilles.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.080
- I think I can handle a little boy

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.940
talks about himself in the third person.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.040
- Who are you? You're weak.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.758
You're despicable, you're disgusting.

00:04:19.758 --> 00:04:23.070
(gasps) You're beautiful!

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.603
Oh, no, what have I done?

00:04:25.603 --> 00:04:28.203
I love you, my Amazon queen!

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.890
But wait, don't I also love Patroclus?

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.740
Why can't these writers
just make up their minds?

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.720
In this story, the Amazon serve

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.060
as little more than
convenient plot devices

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.880
designed to bolster the
reputation of Greece's military

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.900
and give their greatest hero
a sharp but romantic edge.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.870
Thanks to misogyny and the
fact that no writing survived

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.690
from anyone who clearly
fit the Amazon description,

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.880
these stories were
dismissed as obvious fiction

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.440
over the next two millennia.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.120
The fearsome Amazons were written off

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.740
as pro-Athens propaganda,

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.290
or reimagined as beardless male foreigners

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.020
who the Greeks mistook
for battleworn women.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.500
That is until a Swiss law
professor named Johann Bachofen

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.810
published a paper in 1861

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.390
making the radically feminist claim

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.190
that Amazons were more than just myth,

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.740
and that early humans
were naturally matriarchal

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.440
before the disease of patriarchy set in.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.380
- But there was no proof
of the Amazons' legitimacy

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:25.680
until the 1970s when
genetic testing technology

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.360
made it possible for archeologists

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.420
to accurately determine
the sex of ancient remains.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.180
Because despite what some
corners of the internet claim,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.240
you cannot tell the sex
of a long-deceased person

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.910
just by looking at the
shape of their skeleton.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.070
In the 1940s, archeologists uncovered

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.010
hundreds of burial mounds called kurgans

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:46.110
in the Great Steppes of eastern
Europe and central Asia,

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:49.920
a region of dry open grasslands
to the northeast of Greece.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.380
The kurgans were left
around the fifth century BCE

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:55.560
by the myriad nomadic tribes
famous for their horse handling

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.460
known to the Greeks as Scythians,
Thracians or Sauromatians.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:00.960
Hey boss, I found a grave site.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:01.793
- What's in it?

00:06:01.793 --> 00:06:04.800
- A bow, some daggers, a cool headpiece.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.690
- It's a male warrior!

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.840
- Do you wanna look at
the actual remains first?

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.160
- Oh no, that'd be silly.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.300
What can those bones tell me

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.513
that the weapons and my rigid
notion of gender roles cannot?

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:20.220
- Originally, the archeologists
assumed the bodies buried

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.880
with weapons were all male
because well, of course they did.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.220
2,500 years after Homer and our scientists

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.440
still couldn't imagine
a society that allowed,

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.750
let alone encouraged their women to fight.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.520
But the genetic testing revealed

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.480
that one third of the kurgans
were eternal resting places

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.600
for female warriors,
buried with weapons, armor,

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.210
and evidence of battle
wounds just like the men.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:45.060
Suddenly the puzzle pieces
started falling into place.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:47.190
Most myths have a kernel of truth in them

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:48.570
if you know how to look for it,

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.130
and this archeological evidence

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.952
was the magnifying glass
scholars had been waiting for.

00:06:52.952 --> 00:06:55.380
Adrienne Mayor, a folklorist and historian

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:56.820
who literally wrote the book

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.430
on separating Amazon fact from fiction,

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:02.220
believes Scythian women were
the real world inspiration

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.484
for ancient Greece's mythical opponents.

00:07:04.484 --> 00:07:06.600
Early artistic depictions of the Amazons

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.850
even showed them wearing Scythian fashion.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.710
- There has never been evidence

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.710
of an all-female society of
warriors across the Black Sea

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.830
who modify their chests and
murder their male children,

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:19.020
but we do know that the
Scythians were active

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:22.530
and roaming near the Black Sea by 900 BCE.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.330
By the time Homer wrote "The Iliad,"

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:26.700
Greeks interacted with
Scythians regularly,

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:28.350
and it's not hard to imagine

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:31.110
how they could have invented
such monstrous figures

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.270
by embellishing some of
their northeastern neighbors'

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.340
more unfamiliar traits.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.368
Homer was an author trying
to write a memorable story,

00:07:37.368 --> 00:07:39.210
after all.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:41.700
The reality of Greek
mythology's favorite war girlies

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.140
is much more mundane.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:45.120
The Great Steppes are a harsh environment

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:46.350
with few resources,

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:49.410
so the Scythians had to
travel and fight to survive.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:50.520
This bred a culture

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.740
where girls learned to
ride their prized horses

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.950
and shoot their innovative
bows just like the boys did.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.470
Still, many of the men
were likely to have died

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:01.110
during hunts or raids,
giving the impression

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:02.850
that there weren't many of them around.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:04.320
- The myth about Amazons killing

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.480
or abandoning their male
children could have arisen

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.180
from the common nomadic
practice of fosterage

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.460
where a family leaves their
son with a neighboring tribe

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.670
for safety, education, or
to strengthen an alliance.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.080
It was also a convenient way

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.910
for the Greeks to
support their false claim

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.980
that Amazons shunned all men.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:22.140
Oh, and that myth about their chests?

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:25.530
That one is just flimflam
from the fifth century BCE.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:26.820
So-called historian Hellanicos

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.130
was trying to derive the
meaning of the word Amazon

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:32.850
by using Greek roots, even
though it wasn't a Greek word.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:35.220
He claimed it meant "without breast,"

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.410
but Amazons were almost never shown

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:39.240
with this particular adaptation in art,

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.990
and even his contemporaries
dismissed his idea as absurd.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.320
The true origin of the word
Amazon remains a mystery.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.290
- So it seems that the brutal
misandrist Amazons of lore

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:50.970
didn't really exist,

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.220
but they were caricatures
of a real culture

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.380
remarkably different from ancient Greece,

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:56.760
one where women weren't relegated

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:59.100
to prim and proper domestic roles.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:00.930
Consider me disappointed, then,

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.330
that Greek writers didn't
include some of the coolest

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:05.640
and most deviant aspects
of Scythian culture

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:08.610
into their monstrous myths,
like the abundant tattoos

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.010
or the fact that most of them
carried hemp-burning kits

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:12.360
in their travel packs.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.770
Most of the world was fooled

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:15.450
by the Amazons' descent into myth,

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:17.670
but there's a tiny island
off the coast of Turkey

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.670
that still honors the
memory of the real Amazons,

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:22.650
women of the Eurasian nomadic tribes

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:25.650
who fought with just as much
bravery and skill as the men.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.990
This Aksu festival on Giresun Island

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:29.340
is celebrated every May

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.070
and heavily features
the sacred Hamza stone,

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.290
which locals believe was
worshiped by the Amazons

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:36.150
who lived there millennia ago.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.250
- [Emily] We can learn so
much from Giresun Island

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.230
about the power of local memory.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.930
Imagine how different the
last 2000 years might've been

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.100
if we had all remembered

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.170
that women can fight ferociously enough

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.240
to give demigod heroes
a run for their money

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:50.280
in even the most biased stories.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.230
Unlike Homer, we can't change history

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:54.210
through our embellishments,
but moving forward,

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:55.410
we can all embody more

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.972
of what Adrienne Mayor
calls the "Amazon spirit."

00:09:57.972 --> 00:09:59.520
Essentially, this is the belief

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.430
that women are equal to
men in courage and skill.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.710
You know, the very message that characters

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.260
like Wonder Woman and Xena
were created to convey.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:09.330
- [Moiya] Now it's time
for the next pantheon pick,

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.580
and I'm excited to
bring some Amazon spirit

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.400
to our utopia with Hippolyta.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.673
- Yes!
- Yes!

00:10:15.673 --> 00:10:19.560
I feel like our utopia
needs some leadership energy

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.570
and it doesn't have to be a monarchy.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.790
We can choose whatever
type of government we want,

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:25.776
but we need someone
who can lead the people

00:10:25.776 --> 00:10:27.660
and if that person can also defend

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:31.320
against any incoming
raids, then that's great.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.153
- Added bonus.

00:10:32.153 --> 00:10:33.930
I think that's a great
pick and I like the idea

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.430
of taking almost like a demigod

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:37.440
or someone more human and adding them

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:39.420
'cause what's utopia without diversity?

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:40.870
- [Moiya] Exactly. I love it.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.780
So that's our pantheon
pick for this episode

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.060
but be sure to tune in
for the rest of the season

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.010
because we will be picking a new deity

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.870
